    {
      "type": "grammar",
      "heading": "How yes/no questions work",
      "body": "Statement: ਤੁਸੀਂ ਠੀਕ ਹੋ — \"you are fine.\"\nQuestion: ਕੀ ਤੁਸੀਂ ਠੀਕ ਹੋ? — \"are you fine?\"\n\nਕੀ at the front marks the sentence as a yes/no question. Nothing else moves — the verb still sits at the end.\n\nIn casual speech people often drop ki and use rising intonation instead — just like \"You're fine?\" in English. For now, using ki is safer and always clear.\n\nAnswering: haan (yes) or nahin (no), optionally followed by the full sentence — \"Haan, main theek haan.\""
    },
    {
      "type": "whyThisWay",
      "heading": "Why is it said this way?",
      "body": "English marks questions by flipping word order or inserting a helper verb (\"do\"). Many learners then expect every language to rearrange sentences for questions — Punjabi doesn't bother.\n\nA leading particle is a common pattern in South Asian languages: the listener hears ki up front and knows a yes/no answer is coming. It is also worth noting that ki does double duty — it also means \"what\" in questions like \"ki hai?\" (what is it?). Context and position tell you which job it is doing."
    },
